-#### Implicit Function Evaluation
-**Definition:** Generates voxel coordinates defined by an implicit function [S2, unofficial].
-**Parameters:**
-*   $f$: function of form $f(x, y, z)$ returning a numeric value [S2, unofficial].
-*   $lower, upper$: range of values considered inside the object. Default: $[-\infty, 0]$. Values $\le 0$ are inside, $> 0$ are outside [S2, unofficial].
-*   $scale$: extra scaling factor applied to coordinates before calling function [S2, unofficial].
-*   $nx, ny, nz$: dimensions of the volume within which the function will be evaluated. Default: $nx=51, ny=nx, nz=nx$ [S2, unofficial].
-**Layout:** Returns a data.frame of coordinates [S2, unofficial].
+*   Degree parameter $i$: A parameter defining the polynomial degree of the kernel [S1, unofficial].
+*   Scale parameter $\sigma$: A parameter scaling the primitive endpoints' radius to define the support of an individual primitive [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Implicit surfaces were introduced as a way to represent complex geometry using a single mathematical function, allowing for smooth blending and deformation [S1][S2, unofficial]. The concept has evolved from simple algebraic surfaces to complex procedural fields and skeleton-based integral surfaces [S1]. Modern implementations leverage GPU compute shaders for real-time evaluation of dense scalar fields, enabling dynamic effects like metaballs and procedural hypertexturing [S1]. The family includes specific kernels like Cauchy, Inverse, and Compact Polynomial for skeleton-based rendering [S1], and tools for voxel generation from arbitrary functions [S2, unofficial].
+An increasingly popular approach uses field functions in 3D, where surfaces are isosurfaces through the field [S1, unofficial]. Surfaces can be easily and smoothly morphed and deformed by slowly changing the attributes of the control primitives [S1, unofficial]. The family includes specific kernels like Cauchy, Inverse, and Compact Polynomial for skeleton-based rendering [S1, unofficial], and tools for voxel generation from arbitrary functions [S2].
 
